course: dictionary
lesson: 15084
title: Many meanings, one word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the context tells you which one you need. «Ripping» has a few. Most often it is the -ing form of «rip», which means tearing quickly and roughly: «Mia was ripping open her birthday present.»
explain: In old British children's books, «ripping» means wonderful or excellent. «We had a ripping time at the fair» means a great time, not a torn one. Ask: does the sentence still make sense if I swap in "tearing"? If not, try "wonderful".
explain: Look at the words around it. «The rip current pulled the swimmer out to sea» is about water moving fast and strongly, and it is dangerous. The words after «ripping» can change the meaning too: «ripping someone off» means cheating them by charging too much.
fact: Ripping is a form of the verb rip: rip, rips, ripped, ripping.
fact: «Ripping» can mean tearing quickly and roughly, as in «ripping open a present».
fact: In old British books, «a ripping yarn» means a thrilling, exciting story.
fact: «Ripping» can also mean moving very fast or strongly, as in «a ripping current».
fact: «Ripping off» is slang for cheating someone by charging too much, or for copying.
exercise: «Mia was ripping open her birthday present.» What is ripping doing here? | *Tearing quickly and roughly | Cheating someone | Wonderful and excellent | Sleeping soundly
exercise: «We had a ripping time at the fair.» What does ripping mean in this sentence? | Moving very fast in the water | Charging too much | Tearing quickly and roughly | *Wonderful and excellent
exercise: «The shop was ripping us off by charging double.» What does ripping off mean? | *Cheating someone by charging too much | Opening something quickly | Moving strongly in the sea | Wonderful and excellent
test: What does «a ripping current» mean? | Charging too much | Wonderful and excellent | Tearing quickly | *Moving very fast and strongly
test: «The old book was a ripping yarn full of pirates.» What does ripping mean? | *Wonderful and exciting | Moving fast in the sea | Cheating someone | Torn in pieces
exam: Ella says «ripping» always means tearing. Is she right? | *No, it can also mean wonderful, very fast or cheating | Yes, ripping always means tearing | No, it only means wonderful | Yes, but only in old books
